WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.467 --> 00:00:03.634
-Welcome back, everyone.
Seth Meyers is joining us today.

00:00:03.634 --> 00:00:04.634
-Great to be here.

00:00:04.634 --> 00:00:06.000
-Ah, thank you for
being here, bud.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.234
-Yeah.

00:00:09.234 --> 00:00:12.033
-During this writers' strike,

00:00:12.033 --> 00:00:14.434
you also started
another podcast.

00:00:14.434 --> 00:00:16.100
-Yes.
-I love this one.

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:17.601
With your brother, Josh.
-Yes.

00:00:17.601 --> 00:00:20.100
-Who I love. And, by the way,
give him my best.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:22.300
-I will. He used to do stuff
with you on the show,

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:23.767
on "Late Night."
-Yes.

00:00:23.767 --> 00:00:25.834
-And, yeah, it's called "Family
Trips with the Meyers Brothers."

00:00:25.834 --> 00:00:28.067
We had this idea for years
and we decided the strike

00:00:28.067 --> 00:00:29.634
was a good time
to get it off the ground.

00:00:29.634 --> 00:00:30.801
-Why "Family Trips"?

00:00:30.801 --> 00:00:33.601
-So, we talked to,
you know, celebrities,

00:00:33.601 --> 00:00:35.434
and we only talked to them

00:00:35.434 --> 00:00:37.267
about the trips they took
when they were kids.

00:00:37.267 --> 00:00:39.067
And it's really fun,
because you realize

00:00:39.067 --> 00:00:40.734
what a great equalizer it is.

00:00:40.734 --> 00:00:43.133
Very few of the celebrities
you know and love

00:00:43.133 --> 00:00:44.767
had money when they were kids.

00:00:44.767 --> 00:00:47.801
And it's just a lot --
And also, you know,

00:00:47.801 --> 00:00:50.667
for Josh and I, we grew up
in the '70s and early '80s,

00:00:50.667 --> 00:00:53.734
and so many people like us
took trips where they were

00:00:53.734 --> 00:00:55.501
just in the back of
a station wagon, no seat belts.

00:00:55.501 --> 00:00:56.734
Nobody even thought
about seat belts.

00:00:56.734 --> 00:00:57.767
-Yeah.

00:00:57.767 --> 00:00:58.868
-The amount of kids --
It's like,

00:00:58.868 --> 00:01:00.334
"My parents
would put down a mattress.

00:01:00.334 --> 00:01:01.834
We had a card table.

00:01:01.834 --> 00:01:03.701
And we'd slide around."

00:01:03.701 --> 00:01:05.834
-Then just random gamblers
back there with the

00:01:05.834 --> 00:01:07.501
strangers you
picked up on the --

00:01:07.501 --> 00:01:09.133
-We'd pick up dealers.
-Yeah.

00:01:09.133 --> 00:01:11.667
Go play poker with my kids.
Yeah, exactly. Smart.

00:01:11.667 --> 00:01:13.467
-But it's fun,
and you just realize --

00:01:13.467 --> 00:01:17.033
Especially
the more trips go wrong,

00:01:17.033 --> 00:01:19.267
I feel like the more
they enter the family lore,

00:01:19.267 --> 00:01:21.467
and those disaster trips
are the ones

00:01:21.467 --> 00:01:23.133
you talk about for years
and years with your family

00:01:23.133 --> 00:01:25.167
because you survived them
and it brought you all closer.

00:01:25.167 --> 00:01:27.334
And so it's nice talking to
people about all those things --

00:01:27.334 --> 00:01:30.400
-Who do you got
coming up on that podcast?

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:31.667
-We got Nate Bargatze.

00:01:31.667 --> 00:01:33.767
I know a huge Nate Bargatze fan
right here.

00:01:33.767 --> 00:01:36.300
-I love that dude.
-Nick Offerman's coming up.

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:37.467
-Mulaney?
-John Mulaney is coming up.

00:01:37.467 --> 00:01:38.667
-Yeah, okay.

00:01:38.667 --> 00:01:41.200
And, so, it's just a --
It's a full delight.

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:43.267
You're going to do it, right?
-I'm so in. I can't wait.

00:01:43.267 --> 00:01:44.734
-We're podcast bros now.
You got to do it.

00:01:44.734 --> 00:01:46.033
-Dude, we have to do it.
-We're podcast bros.

00:01:46.033 --> 00:01:47.901
-I already got my trip
planned out. I can't wait.

00:01:47.901 --> 00:01:50.067
-Great.
-I have all the gear.

00:01:50.067 --> 00:01:51.234
-Yeah, you do have
all the gear.

00:01:51.234 --> 00:01:52.267
-What am I going to do
with all this gear?

00:01:52.267 --> 00:01:54.200
-I know.
-Kimmel was like -- He's like,

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:57.901
"If you're doing this podcast,
I already ordered this mixer,

00:01:57.901 --> 00:02:00.100
these headphones,
I got this microphone thing."

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:01.934
I go, "Oh, my gosh."
I don't know what --

00:02:01.934 --> 00:02:03.133
But he gave us, like,
an Amazon link.

00:02:03.133 --> 00:02:05.334
-I wanted to talk about --
I almost forgot.

00:02:05.334 --> 00:02:06.801
The best thing about
"Strike Force Five" is,

00:02:06.801 --> 00:02:08.834
we all -- Like,
we all got better as we went,

00:02:08.834 --> 00:02:11.501
because the first room --
You did the first two episodes

00:02:11.501 --> 00:02:14.334
in a room, and I've never seen
Jimmy look hotter.

00:02:14.334 --> 00:02:17.033
He chose -- And, again,
we started in August.

00:02:17.033 --> 00:02:19.033
There was terrible ventilation,

00:02:19.033 --> 00:02:21.100
and just it was like
watching you melt.

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:23.133
-Well, I didn't know what room
to do the podcast in.

00:02:23.133 --> 00:02:24.267
Nothing was really quiet.

00:02:24.267 --> 00:02:25.367
And so I went --

00:02:25.367 --> 00:02:28.801
We have, like, a guest cottage.

00:02:28.801 --> 00:02:31.734
So I went to this cottage thing,
and there was a loud cricket

00:02:31.734 --> 00:02:33.767
that was so loud, I go,
"They're going to think --"

00:02:33.767 --> 00:02:36.133
And I would have to stomp
on the floor,

00:02:36.133 --> 00:02:37.634
and then it would be quiet.

00:02:37.634 --> 00:02:39.300
I go, "I can't do this
for the whole --"

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:41.234
-Wait. The cricket would be
quiet if you stomped?

00:02:41.234 --> 00:02:43.367
So it was a very --
It was a respectful cricket.

00:02:43.367 --> 00:02:45.234
-Trained cricket. Yeah.

00:02:45.234 --> 00:02:47.100
I don't want to work
with the amateur crickets.

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:49.901
Yeah. He's famous.
He might -- Jiminy?

00:02:49.901 --> 00:02:51.167
-Oh, Jiminy?!
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:02:51.167 --> 00:02:52.801
He's pretty famous.
He's been in a couple films.

00:02:52.801 --> 00:02:54.200
-Oh, my God!
-Yeah, no big deal.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:56.400
I don't want to name-drop,
but I hang out with cartoons.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:58.801
-Wow!
-You don't know me?

00:02:58.801 --> 00:03:01.033
My whole world is car--
-That was the Jiminy Cricket?

00:03:01.033 --> 00:03:02.567
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
We're old, old, old buddies.

00:03:02.567 --> 00:03:04.200
-And you didn't want to
bring him on the podcast?

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:06.534
-Yeah, what is he going to talk
about? Pinocchio?

00:03:06.534 --> 00:03:08.367
-Yes!
-Nah, come on. Come on.

00:03:08.367 --> 00:03:09.701
He's not in the new one.

00:03:09.701 --> 00:03:12.968
So, I went to this room,
and there was

00:03:12.968 --> 00:03:14.968
no air-conditioning,
and I was just shoved in

00:03:14.968 --> 00:03:16.367
so I could get out
of everyone's way.

00:03:16.367 --> 00:03:18.167
And it was so hot.
-Yeah.

00:03:18.167 --> 00:03:20.634
-And I go, "Oh, my gosh."
And we did two episodes.

00:03:20.634 --> 00:03:21.734
And we were just trying to
figure out --

00:03:21.734 --> 00:03:23.133
So, I think it
was like four hours.

00:03:23.133 --> 00:03:25.033
-It was long.
The first day was long.

00:03:25.033 --> 00:03:26.934
-But then here's
why I was -- I wasn't mad,

00:03:26.934 --> 00:03:28.000
but it was making me laugh.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:29.300
Everything was making me laugh.

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:30.534
But because I was like,
"I got to get out of here,

00:03:30.534 --> 00:03:31.634
because I'm going to die."

00:03:31.634 --> 00:03:33.133
I mean, my face was red.

00:03:33.133 --> 00:03:35.634
I was -- So, I texted everybody
on Strike Force.

00:03:35.634 --> 00:03:36.767
-So, this is the craziest thing.

00:03:36.767 --> 00:03:39.267
So, Jimmy is talking
to four of us on --

00:03:39.267 --> 00:03:41.701
It's not a live podcast,

00:03:41.701 --> 00:03:43.767
so anything he says
we could just edit out.

00:03:43.767 --> 00:03:45.200
-I know, I know, I know, I know.

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:48.534
-But, instead,
he chooses to text us.

00:03:48.534 --> 00:03:50.000
-Well, I didn't think
you were gonna read it,

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:52.567
so I texted everyone, "Hey,
I got to get out of here, man."

00:03:52.567 --> 00:03:55.567
Like, "Wrap it up.
Let's go, man."

00:03:55.567 --> 00:03:57.567
I'm like -- And then --

00:03:57.567 --> 00:03:58.801
-Colbert immediately was like,

00:03:58.801 --> 00:04:02.734
"Hey, I just got a text
from Jimmy.

00:04:02.734 --> 00:04:06.567
He's got to go."
And we're like, "What?"

00:04:06.567 --> 00:04:09.501
It'd be like if you were at
dinner with one other person

00:04:09.501 --> 00:04:11.200
and you texted them,

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:15.334
"How much longer
is this dinner going to go?"

00:04:15.334 --> 00:04:18.300
-Oh, my God.
Totally busted me on that.

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:21.067
So, you and Oliver
are doing stand-up together.

00:04:21.067 --> 00:04:23.534
-Yeah, John Oliver and I --
we're going to do

00:04:23.534 --> 00:04:25.467
New Year's Eve in Las Vegas.

00:04:25.467 --> 00:04:27.000
-Whoo!
-Very exciting.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:29.300
-That's going to be fun.
-And I'm also very excited.

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:32.467
I'm doing a fundraising show
in New Hampshire, in Manchester.

00:04:32.467 --> 00:04:34.000
That's my home state.
-That's right.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:36.834
-It's a fundraiser for a couple
of great organizations --

00:04:36.834 --> 00:04:39.167
Granite State Children's
Alliance and CASA.

00:04:39.167 --> 00:04:41.067
So if you live
in the Granite State,

00:04:41.067 --> 00:04:43.000
come check it out, because
it's a really special night.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:44.100
-You're a good man.

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:45.634
We always love it
when you stop by.

00:04:45.634 --> 00:04:47.300
That's my "Strike Force Five"
buddy right there.

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:48.567
Seth Meyers, everybody.
-Here we go!

00:04:48.567 --> 00:04:49.767
-Stay tuned.

00:04:49.767 --> 00:04:52.467
"Late Night with Seth Meyers"
airs weeknights,

00:04:52.467 --> 00:04:55.400
including Wednesdays,
right after "The Tonight Show."

00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:57.067
It's also available on Peacock.

00:04:57.067 --> 00:04:58.634
Stick around.
We'll be right back, everybody.

